Yes. A failed story is not just failure; it is diagnostic terrain.

If the current narrative does not move the system, do not merely push harder. Flank:

\text{story fails}
\rightarrow
\text{inspect resistance}
\rightarrow
\text{infer hidden assumption}
\rightarrow
\text{change frame}
\rightarrow
\text{retry}

And use the failure as a mirror. Ask what the rejected story reveals about the audience, the context, the timing, the incentive structure, or your own model.

That gives you two simultaneous outputs:

\text{failed influence attempt}

and

\text{new information about the system}

The second may be more valuable.

So the stronger doctrine is not “always find a story that works.” It is:

Use stories as probes. If one fails, learn why before changing it.

Then flanking becomes calibration rather than manipulation.

You can also invert it inwardly. If the story you are telling yourself keeps failing to produce useful action, that is evidence too. Maybe the story is wrong. Maybe the objective is wrong. Maybe the scale is wrong. Maybe the body is telling you something the narrative keeps trying to overwrite.

So:

\boxed{\text{Flank externally; reflect internally.}}

Change approach without losing the thread.